    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find \\(ab + ac + bc\\) for the roots \\(a, b, c\\) of the polynomial \\(3x^3 - 3x^2 + 11x - 8 = 0\\), we start by using Vieta's formulas. Vieta's formulas relate the coefficients of a polynomial to sums and products of its roots.\n\nGiven the polynomial \\(3x^3 - 3x^2 + 11x - 8 = 0\\), we identify the coefficients:\n- \\(a\\) is the root corresponding to the coefficient of \\(x^2\\) with a negative sign, which is \\(-3\\).\n- \\(b\\) and \\(c\\) are the roots corresponding to the other coefficients.\n- \\(a + b + c = -\\frac{\\text{coefficient of } x^2}{\\text{leading coefficient}} = -\\frac{-3}{3} = 1\\).\n- \\(ab + ac + bc = \\frac{\\text{coefficient of } x}{\\text{leading coefficient}} = \\frac{11}{3}\\).\n- \\(abc = \\frac{\\text{constant term}}{\\text{leading coefficient}} = \\frac{-8}{3}\\).\n\nWe need to find \\(ab + ac + bc\\). From Vieta's formulas, we know:\n\\[\nab + ac + bc = \\frac{11}{3}.\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(ab + ac + bc\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{11}{3}}\\).",
